/*
PROJECT: Splunk Authentication Investigation
INDEX: main
DATASET: small_company_auth_lab_v2

PURPOSE:
Investigate authentication activity, identify suspicious failed-login
patterns, examine targeted accounts, and identify potential follow-up
detections.

DATA NOTE:
This project uses synthetic training data. The documented IP addresses
are reserved for documentation and are not evidence of real-world attackers.

HOW TO USE:
Each numbered section is a separate SPL query.
Run one query at a time in Splunk.
*/

/*
============================================================
QUERY 01 - Authentication Event Overview
============================================================

PURPOSE:
Display authentication events and their relevant fields.

WHY THIS LOGIC:
The table command selects the fields needed for investigation.
Sorting by descending event time places the newest events first.
Filtering by index and dataset keeps the search focused on this lab.

WHAT THE RESULTS SHOW:
A chronological overview of authentication events, including usernames,
source IPs, outcomes, authentication methods, MFA results, and hostnames.
This provides context for the subsequent investigation.

INTERPRETATION LIMITATION:
The event overview alone does not establish malicious activity.
*/

index=main dataset="small_company_auth_lab_v2"
| table _time username source_ip status event_id message department auth_method mfa_result hostname
| sort - _time


/*
============================================================
QUERY 02 - Failed Logins by Source IP and Username
============================================================

PURPOSE:
Measure failed authentication attempts for each source IP and account.

WHY THIS LOGIC:
count calculates the number of failed events for each IP-user pair.
min(_time) and max(_time) identify the first and last observed attempts.
Grouping by both source_ip and username helps distinguish activity
against individual accounts from activity involving multiple accounts.

WHAT THE RESULTS SHOW:
A list of IP-account pairs ranked by failed-attempt count, with the
observed time range for each pair. Repeated failures may warrant
additional investigation.

INTERPRETATION LIMITATION:
Repeated failures can also result from mistyped passwords,
misconfigured applications, or legitimate user activity.
*/

index=main dataset="small_company_auth_lab_v2" status="failed"
| stats count AS failed_attempts min(_time) AS first_attempt max(_time) AS last_attempt BY source_ip username
| convert ctime(first_attempt) ctime(last_attempt)
| sort - failed_attempts


/*
============================================================
QUERY 03 - Failed Logins and Targeted Accounts by IP
============================================================

PURPOSE:
Identify source IPs associated with failed logins against multiple accounts.

WHY THIS LOGIC:
count measures failed events per IP.
dc(username) counts distinct targeted accounts.
values(username) lists the usernames observed for each IP.
The time fields show the earliest and latest observed failures.
Sorting by targeted account count and then failed attempts prioritizes
IPs that may warrant closer review.

WHAT THE RESULTS SHOW:
A per-IP summary of failed attempts, distinct targeted accounts,
observed usernames, and time range. Activity against many accounts
can be consistent with a password-spraying pattern.

INTERPRETATION LIMITATION:
This query summarizes the entire selected dataset time range.
It does not enforce a short time window, so it is not by itself
a time-bounded password-spraying detection rule.
*/

index=main dataset="small_company_auth_lab_v2" status="failed"
| stats count AS failed_attempts dc(username) AS targeted_users values(username) AS usernames min(_time) AS first_attempt max(_time) AS last_attempt BY source_ip
| convert ctime(first_attempt) ctime(last_attempt)
| sort - targeted_users - failed_attempts


/*
============================================================
QUERY 04 - Detailed Events from Suspicious Source IP
============================================================

PURPOSE:
Inspect the authentication events associated with 203.0.113.77.

WHY THIS LOGIC:
Filtering by source_ip narrows the search to one source.
The selected fields retain the event time, account, outcome,
event details, authentication method, MFA result, and hostname.
Sorting in ascending time order makes the sequence easier to review.

WHAT THE RESULTS SHOW:
The chronological authentication events associated with this IP,
including failed and successful outcomes if present in the data.

INTERPRETATION LIMITATION:
203.0.113.77 is a documentation-range IP used in this synthetic lab.
It must not be presented as a real external attacker.
*/

index=main dataset="small_company_auth_lab_v2" source_ip="203.0.113.77"
| table _time username source_ip status event_id message auth_method mfa_result hostname
| sort _time


/*
============================================================
QUERY 05 - Authentication Summary for Targeted Accounts
============================================================

PURPOSE:
Compare failed and successful authentication events for accounts
that had failed attempts from 203.0.113.77.

WHY THIS LOGIC:
The subsearch selects usernames associated with failed events from
the chosen source IP. The outer search then summarizes all matching
authentication events for those usernames in the selected dataset.
Conditional counts separate failed and successful events.
The first and last successful event times provide additional context.

WHAT THE RESULTS SHOW:
For each selected account, the total failed and successful events
and the earliest and latest successful event times observed in the
dataset. These details help prioritize accounts for further review.

INTERPRETATION LIMITATION:
The summary does not prove that a successful login came from the
same source IP. It also does not prove that a successful event
was caused by a preceding failed attempt.
*/

index=main dataset="small_company_auth_lab_v2"
[
    search index=main dataset="small_company_auth_lab_v2" source_ip="203.0.113.77" status="failed"
    | stats count BY username
    | fields username
]
| stats
    count(eval(status="failed")) AS failed_logins
    count(eval(status="success")) AS successful_logins
    min(eval(if(status="success", _time, null()))) AS first_success
    max(eval(if(status="success", _time, null()))) AS last_success
    BY username
| eval first_success=if(isnull(first_success), "No successful login", strftime(first_success, "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S"))
| eval last_success=if(isnull(last_success), "No successful login", strftime(last_success, "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S"))
| table username failed_logins successful_logins first_success last_success
| sort - failed_logins


/*
============================================================
QUERY 06 - Failed Event Immediately Followed by Success
============================================================

PURPOSE:
Find successful events occurring within one hour of the immediately
preceding failed event for the same username, for accounts targeted
by failed attempts from 203.0.113.77.

WHY THIS LOGIC:
The subsearch selects accounts that had failed attempts from the
chosen source. sort 0 _time orders their events chronologically.
streamstats retrieves the preceding event's status and timestamp
for each username. The where condition selects a success whose
immediately preceding event was a failure no more than 3600 seconds
earlier.

WHAT THE RESULTS SHOW:
Matching username-event sequences, the previous event time, the
successful event time, and the elapsed seconds between them.
These matches can help analysts decide which events to investigate.

INTERPRETATION LIMITATION:
The immediately preceding failure may have come from another source.
This query does not establish that the success came from
203.0.113.77 or that an account was compromised. It can also miss
a relevant failure if another event occurs between the failure
and success.
*/

index=main dataset="small_company_auth_lab_v2"
[
    search index=main dataset="small_company_auth_lab_v2" source_ip="203.0.113.77" status="failed"
    | stats count BY username
    | fields username
]
| sort 0 _time
| streamstats current=f last(status) AS previous_status last(_time) AS previous_time BY username
| eval seconds_since_previous=_time-previous_time
| where status="success" AND previous_status="failed" AND seconds_since_previous>=0 AND seconds_since_previous<=3600
| eval seconds_since_previous=round(seconds_since_previous,0)
| table username previous_time _time seconds_since_previous source_ip status hostname auth_method mfa_result
| sort seconds_since_previous


/*
============================================================
QUERY 07 - Authentication Summary for a Specific Source IP
============================================================

PURPOSE:
Summarize the total authentication activity associated with
203.0.113.77.

WHY THIS LOGIC:
count calculates all matching events.
Conditional counts separate successful and failed logins.
dc(username) counts the distinct accounts represented in the events.
The final table presents a compact summary for reporting.

WHAT THE RESULTS SHOW:
The total observed events, number of distinct usernames,
failed logins, and successful logins associated with this source.

INTERPRETATION LIMITATION:
This query covers the selected dataset time range and is a summary,
not a time-windowed alert. The targeted account count can include
accounts with any recorded outcome, not only failed attempts.
*/

index=main dataset="small_company_auth_lab_v2" source_ip="203.0.113.77"
| stats
    count AS total_attempts
    count(eval(status="success")) AS successful_logins
    count(eval(status="failed")) AS failed_logins
    dc(username) AS targeted_accounts
    BY source_ip
| table source_ip total_attempts targeted_accounts failed_logins successful_logins
